Umbral Swing

Creative Catalyst: It descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

Umbral Swing is the sound of a carousel slowing to a halt as the sky turns to bruised violet. It marries the frenetic, syncopated bounce of early 20th-century swing with the grounding, expansive frequencies of 432Hz meditative soundscapes. The Hohner Clavinet provides the percussive, biting grit, processed through tape-saturation and slow-moving tremolo to create a sound that feels simultaneously urgent and suspended in time.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 90–115 BPM; a "limping" swing shuffle that feels like a heartbeat under extreme duress.
*   Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Mixolydian modes tuned to A=432Hz, favoring dissonant "blue" notes that resolve into surprisingly pure, resonant intervals.
*   Production Signatures: Heavy use of natural plate reverb to simulate grand tent acoustics; granular synthesis applied to percussion; side-chaining the Clavinet against an orchestral sub-bass.
*   Dynamics: Starts with a sparse, rhythmic focus, blooming into chaotic, lush brass-and-synth swells before collapsing back into near-silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
The Umbral Swing movement emerged from the "Black Sun Circuit," a clandestine group of nomadic carnival performers who only set up their midway rigs during total solar eclipses. Clad in velvet-lined dusters and brass-goggled spectacles, these musicians believe the occlusion of the sun releases a specific "harmonic radiation" that their instruments capture. They operate out of pop-up tent cities in remote deserts, where the lack of light pollution turns their performances into sensory-deprivation rituals. It is a subculture of horologists and avant-garde jazz heads, characterized by jewelry made of corroded copper and a strict vow of silence between performances.

Central Instruments: Hohner Clavinet (heavily processed), detuned upright piano, analog tape delay, brass section, 432Hz resonant gongs.

